window.docContents[40].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence--Constructing-Characteristic-Families":{"contents":"Picard–Lindelöf constructs the phase-space trajectories and continuous\ndependence supplies a common family over nearby boundary parameters.\nConstraint preservation then keeps compatible initial states on the\nhypersurface .\n\nIf the uncurried Hamiltonian is , Picard–Lindelöf constructs, through\nevery phase-space state , a local curve satisfying\n\nThe  Hamiltonian makes the characteristic vector field continuously\ndifferentiable and therefore locally Lipschitz. Picard–Lindelöf supplies a\nunique local integral curve through , with the initial condition and\nODE identities built into the resulting flow.\n\nA single trajectory is not enough; the construction needs the trajectories\nthrough all boundary states near  on a common time interval, jointly\ncontinuous in the parameter. Continuous dependence on initial conditions\nsupplies precisely this.\n\nNear any base parameter, a  phase-space Hamiltonian and a  boundary-state family\n give a family , jointly continuous for short times, withProjecting to components gives Evans's three equations for , , and\n. The initial states need not be compatible.\n\nApply local existence with continuous dependence to the initial states\n near the base parameter, shrinking to one common time interval.\nThe resulting flow composed with  is jointly continuous, and its time\nderivative is the characteristic vector field; projecting to the three\ncomponents gives Evans's equations.\n\nAdding compatibility of the initial gradients on a neighborhood, the first\nintegral  vanishes at the start of every characteristic in the family, and\nits invariance keeps the whole family on the constraint hypersurface.\n\nRetain the  Hamiltonian and  boundary-state hypotheses, and\nsuppose the initial gradient is compatible with the boundary data throughout\na neighborhood of the base parameter. Then there is a continuous family of\ncharacteristics that starts from the prescribed phase states, solves the full\ncharacteristic vector-field ODE, and satisfiesat every point of the local flow.\n\nConstruct the common local characteristic family, shrink its parameter set so\nthe compatibility condition holds everywhere, and apply uniqueness for the\nscalar ODE satisfied by the residual along each characteristic.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Nonlinear First-Order Equations\u0009Characteristics and Local Existence","header":"7.4.1.2. Constructing Characteristic Families","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence--Constructing-Characteristic-Families"}});